 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "L7Y56FkzteUJ",
   "from": "wooster",
   "author": "ac7693735df0b19b",
   "date": "2011-01-28T11:31:06Z",
   "subject": "ordering of weekdays on x axis",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Hello --\r<br>\r<br>I need to plot x=weekday, y=count from a data frame called &#39;result&#39;:\r<br>\r<br>weekdays     med\r<br>MON 56260.0\r<br>TUE 56814.0\r<br>WED 63163.0\r<br>THU 52629.0\r<br>FRI 48006.0\r<br>SAT 32809.5\r<br>SUN 39459.5\r<br>\r<br>I try a bar chart:\r<br>p &lt;- ggplot(result,aes(weekdays,med))\r<br>p + layer(geom=&quot;bar&quot;)\r<br>\r<br>But the x-axis plots the weekdays as follows: F, M, Sa, Su, Th, Tu, W\r<br>\r<br>Can anyone shed any light on why this happens and how to override it?\r<br>\r<br>thank you in advance, Andrew\r<br>ggplot2 rules btw!"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "19pXiocmhaUJ",
   "from": "Scott Chamberlain",
   "author": "9ff02ee016b8a9e0",
   "date": "2011-01-28T12:59:58Z",
   "subject": "Re: ordering of weekdays on x axis",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div>\n            <div><span>This is not an elegant solution, but you could simply do this, reordering day by the column &#39;order&#39;:</span></div><div><span><br></span></div><div><span><p style=\"margin:0.0px 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px;font:12.0px Monaco;color:#b0130a\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">result</span><span style=\"color:#0e1f99\"> &lt;- </span><span style=\"color:#000000\">data.frame</span><span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000000\">day</span><span style=\"color:#0e1f99\"> = </span><span style=\"color:#000000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">(</span>&quot;Mon&quot;<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">, </span>&quot;Tue&quot;<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">, </span>&quot;Wed&quot;<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">, </span>&quot;Thu&quot;<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">, </span>&quot;Fri&quot;<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">, </span>&quot;Sat&quot;<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">, </span>&quot;Sun&quot;<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">), </span></p>\n<p style=\"margin:0.0px 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px;font:12.0px Monaco;color:#055218\"><span style=\"color:#0e1f99\"><span style=\"white-space:pre\">\t</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\">count</span><span style=\"color:#0e1f99\"> = </span><span style=\"color:#000000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">(</span>56260<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">, </span>56814<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">, </span>63163<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">, </span>52629<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">, </span>48006<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">, </span>32809<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">, </span>39459<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">), </span></p>\n<p style=\"margin:0.0px 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px;font:12.0px Monaco;color:#0e1f99\"><span style=\"white-space:pre\">\t</span><span style=\"color:#000000\">order</span> = <span style=\"color:#000000\">seq</span>(<span style=\"color:#055218\">1</span>:<span style=\"color:#055218\">7</span>))</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0.0px 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px;font:12.0px Monaco;color:#0e1f99;min-height:16.0px\"><br></p>\n<p style=\"margin:0.0px 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px;font:13.0px Helvetica\">ggplot<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">(</span>result<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">, </span>aes<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">(</span>reorder<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">(</span>day<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">, </span>order<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">), </span>count<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">)) + </span></p>\n<p style=\"margin:0.0px 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px;font:13.0px Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#0e1f99\"><span style=\"white-space:pre\">\t</span></span>geom_bar<span style=\"color:#0e1f99\">()</span></p>\n<p style=\"margin:0.0px 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px;font:13.0px Helvetica;color:#0e1f99;min-height:16.0px\"><span style=\"white-space:pre\">\t</span></p><div><span style=\"white-space:pre\"><br></span></div>\n                </span></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div><div>\n                <span></span>\n                \n                <p style=\"color:#a0a0a0\">On Friday, January 28, 2011 at 5:31 AM, wooster wrote:</p>\n                </div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\" style=\"border-left-style:solid;border-width:1px;margin-left:0px;padding-left:10px\"><span><div><div>Hello --<br><br>I need to plot x=weekday, y=count from a data frame called &#39;result&#39;:<br><br>weekdays     med<br>MON 56260.0<br>TUE 56814.0<br>WED 63163.0<br>THU 52629.0<br>FRI 48006.0<br>SAT 32809.5<br>SUN 39459.5<br><br>I try a bar chart:<br>p &lt;- ggplot(result,aes(weekdays,med))<br>p + layer(geom=&quot;bar&quot;)<br><br>But the x-axis plots the weekdays as follows: F, M, Sa, Su, Th, Tu, W<br><br>Can anyone shed any light on why this happens and how to override it?<br><br>thank you in advance, Andrew<br>ggplot2 rules btw!<br><br></div></div></span></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\" style=\"border-left-style:solid;border-width:1px;margin-left:0px;padding-left:10px\"><span><div><div>-- <br>You received this message because you are subscribed to the ggplot2 mailing list.<br>Please provide a reproducible example: <a href=\"http://gist.github.com/270442\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://gist.github.com/270442&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792380810083000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3w2hpY1r0JS9URT30NzwTQ\">http://gist.github.com/270442</a><br><br>To post: email <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggp...@googlegroups.com</a><br>To unsubscribe: email <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggplot2+u...@googlegroups.com</a><br>More options: <a href=\"http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792380810084000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0lyqrh9HqDSJBvIEws7Ci5\">http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2</a><br></div></div></span>\n                \n                \n                \n                \n                </blockquote>\n                \n                <div>\n                    <br>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>"
